## Evidence model

Command execution writes to `.cargo-cicd/`:

| Path | Contents |
|------|----------|
| `.cargo-cicd/receipts/` | blake3-hashed JSON receipts, one per verdict; `latest.json` points at the newest |
| `.cargo-cicd/ocel/events.jsonl` | OCEL 2.0 event log (XES is no longer an export format) |

Receipts record command, verdict, timing, and artifact hashes. `receipt
verify` recomputes blake3 digests from disk and fails on any mismatch.

Fail-closed semantics: a `FAIL` verdict exits the process non-zero. A missing
external tool is a typed failure, not a warning (enforced by
`tests/fail_closed_exit_semantics.rs` — real subprocesses, real exit codes).

<!-- BEGIN custom:cicd-toml -->
## Release discipline

Version parity between `Cargo.toml` and the release surface is enforced by a
generated court (`tests/version_parity_proof.rs`). Release gates are measured
from real artifacts, not asserted. The publish-candidate workflow
(`.github/workflows/publish-candidate.yml`, generated) stages artifacts;
actual `publish` and `git close` actions that touch remotes remain
user-gated on the operator's machine.

## cicd.toml

`cargo-cicd` publishes workspace state, policy verdicts, and the append-only
event log to `cicd.toml` in the workspace root.
<!-- END custom:cicd-toml -->

## Limitations

- Full audit verdicts require the optional `wpm`/`wasm4pm` oracle on `PATH`;
  without it, conformance verdicts degrade to a typed Blocked/Partial result
  rather than a pass.
- External tools (`cyclonedx`, `cargo semver-checks`, `just`, `act`, `gh`)
  are discovered on `PATH`; absence yields a typed Unavailable failure for
  the verbs that need them.
- Anti-cheat scanning (LLM-contamination checks, fixture tamper detection)
  is heuristic. It raises the cost of gaming the gates; it does not make
  gaming impossible.

